{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. He became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his youth a regency was established under his mother and later under his uncle Joscelin II of Edessa. Raymond spent much of his early years under the influence of powerful nobles and church figures who sought to control the county of Tripoli. He was educated in the ways of governance and warfare which prepared him for his future role as a key leader in the Levant.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a crucial role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a close ally and sometimes rival of the kings of Jerusalem. He was appointed regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of King Baldwin III. He held this position during the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was known for his cautious diplomacy and often sought peaceful resolutions to conflicts with Muslim powers. He was a key negotiator with Saladin and was involved in several truces. His approach contrasted with the more aggressive stance of other nobles such as the Knights Templar. Raymond was also instrumental in the succession disputes that followed the death of Baldwin IV and the rise of Baldwin V.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III faced numerous military challenges during his rule. He was captured by Muslim forces in 1164 and spent several years in captivity. His release was secured through a large ransom and territorial concessions. Upon his return he worked to restore the strength of Tripoli and rebuild its defenses. He was involved in the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where he fought alongside Guy of Lusignan. After the defeat he retreated to Tripoli and died shortly after in October of that year. His death marked the end of an era of cautious leadership in the Crusader states. Raymond III is remembered as a pragmatic nobleman who tried to preserve Christian holdings through diplomacy rather than endless warfare. His legacy is complex as he was both praised for his restraint and criticized for his indecisiveness. His rule reflected the fragile state of the Crusader territories in the late twelfth century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "